  "summary": "404 Media obtained body-camera and in-car footage from the Tulsa County, Oklahoma case in which U.S. District Judge Sara Hill ruled that Deputy Freddie Alaniz's Flock and Motorola ALPR searches of Melissa Kyle's rental car constituted an unconstitutional warrantless search under the Fourth Amendment. The footage shows Alaniz running the plate on Flock solely because of its California origin, then using ALPR travel history to interrogate Kyle during a traffic stop that led to the discovery of 91 pounds of meth\u2014evidence the judge ordered suppressed.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "I saw her go by, and I just started running her info on Flock and Vigilant",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "The Court finds that because the ALPR systems Alaniz used to search Kyle's historical location information intruded on her reasonable expectation of privacy in the whole of her physical movements, it was a search under the Fourth Amendment.",
      "paragraph_idx": 24
    },
    {
      "quote": "In a five-day period, the car was scanned more than 50 times by 11 different law enforcement agencies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 23
    }
  ],
